# Line endings are settled HERE, not per-shell and not per-editor.
#
# Why this file exists: 27 of the kit's text files had MIXED line endings - CRLF and LF in the same file -
# because edits written with CRLF strings landed in LF files. That is not a shell problem; every one of
# those edits happened inside a single shell. What was missing was a normalization rule and a test.
#
# A "line-ending style" setting in the kit could not have prevented any of it: the tools that read these
# files (git, .NET's regex engine, Node reading settings.json, cmd.exe parsing a .cmd) do not consult kit
# settings. git's attributes are the mechanism that actually governs this, so this is where the rule lives.
#
# Consequences of a mixed file, all observed in this project:
#   - .NET multiline regex: `^...$` anchors match before \n, NOT before \r\n, so a pattern that works on
#     the LF half silently fails on the CRLF half of the same file.
#   - a literal string replace built with "`r`n" matches nothing in an LF region, so the edit no-ops -
#     which is how the README's H1 got mangled and how 7 requirements got glued onto previous lines.

# Default: normalize on commit, LF in the working tree. PowerShell, .NET, Node and git all read LF fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# EXCEPT batch files. cmd.exe has historically misparsed LF-only batch (labels and goto in particular).
# It happens to work on current Windows 11, but this kit installs on other people's machines and the cost
# of being conservative here is zero.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.onnx binary
